21. Non-Functional Requirements
Functional requirements describe what the system does. Non-functional requirements describe the quality characteristics expected from the system.
---
21.1 Security
NFR-001
User credentials shall be securely handled and stored.
NFR-002
Protected resources shall require appropriate authentication and authorization.
NFR-003
Sensitive configuration values such as database credentials and API credentials shall not be stored directly in source code.
NFR-004
The system shall follow appropriate web-security practices.
---
21.2 Performance
NFR-005
The application should provide responsive interaction for normal user operations.
NFR-006
The system should avoid unnecessary external API requests.
NFR-007
Database queries should be designed to avoid unnecessary data retrieval.
---
21.3 Reliability
NFR-008
The application should handle temporary external API failures gracefully.
NFR-009
A failure in retrieving external data should not unnecessarily make the entire application unavailable.
NFR-010
Stored user data should remain consistent after synchronization operations.
---
21.4 Usability
NFR-011
The application should provide a clear and understandable user interface.
NFR-012
The dashboard should present important information without requiring unnecessary navigation.
NFR-013
The application should provide meaningful feedback when an operation succeeds or fails.
---
21.5 Responsiveness
NFR-014
The application should be usable on desktop, tablet, and mobile screen sizes.
---
21.6 Maintainability
NFR-015
The application shall use a modular architecture.
NFR-016
Individual application modules should have clearly defined responsibilities.
NFR-017
The source code should follow consistent coding and naming conventions.
NFR-018
Important application functionality should be covered by automated tests.
---
21.7 Scalability
NFR-019
The architecture should allow additional competitive-programming platforms to be integrated without major restructuring.
NFR-020
The database design should support growth in the number of users and stored coding records.
